er Collaboration L29:   6. cite19†5 Conclusion L30:   7. cite20†References L31:   8. cite21†A Related Work L32:     1. cite22†A.1 Long-Horizon CLI Agent Evaluation L33:     2. cite23†A.2 User-Interactive CLI Agent Evaluation L34:   9. cite24†B Details of Collaboration Framework L35:     1. cite25†B.1 Taxonomy of Collaboration Dimensions L36:     2. cite26†B.2 Anatomy of Scenario Templates L37:     3. cite27†B.3 Evidence-Based Collaboration Evaluation L38:   10. cite28†C Details of the RealCLI Benchmark L39:     1. cite29†C.1 Details of Task Taxonomy L40:     2. cite30†C.2 Details of Benchmark Instances L41:       1. cite31†Instance Layout. L42:       2. cite32†Interaction Script. L43:       3. cite33†Runtime Trace. L44:   11. cite34†D Experimental Setup Details L45:     1. cite35†D.1 Details of Agent Harness L46:       1. cite36†Budgets and timeouts. L47:     2. cite37†D.2 Details of Models L48:     3. cite38†D.3 Details of User Simulator L49:       1. cite39†Operational constraints. L50:       2. cite40†Simulated-User Guide. L51:     4. cite41†D.4 Details of Evaluation Judge L52:       1. cite42†Judge Prompt Template. L53:   12. cite43†E Supplementary Analysis L54:     1. cite44†E.1 User Simulator Control and Trace Auditing L55:     2. cite45†E.2 Interaction Reorders Task Success L56:     3. cite46†E.3 Long-Horizon Interaction Costs and Outcomes L57:     4. cite47†E.4 Immediate Response and Cross-Turn Goal Maintenance L58:     5. cite48†E.5 Acceptance Gaps Under Complete Instructions L59:     6. cite49†E.6 Task Success and Collaboration Success L60:     7. cite50†E.7 From Stated Constraints to Delivered Artifacts L61: cite51†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L62: \nL63: arXiv:2610.22507v1 [cs.CL] 15 Oct 2026\nL64: \nL65: ^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{*} Equal contribution.   ^{†} Corresponding author.\nL66: # RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation of\nL67: Human–Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents\nL68: Yunqi Liu^{3*}  Peiyu Wang^{3*}  Bingli Wang^{3*}  Leo Luo^{3*}  Xuanwu Song^{3*} Xudong Ma^{3}  Hongxin Li^{3}  Yaxin Du^{3}  Weicheng Gu^{3}  Fanglin Xu^{3}  Yi Xing^{3} XiangLong Liu^{1}  Jujie He^{3}  Zhange Zhang^{3}  Huatong Song^{3}  Zhaoyi Li^{3} Deyang Kong^{3}  Chuan Hao^{3}  Peihao Wu^{3}  Mingjie Tang^{3}  Ran Tao^{3} Jian Yang^{1†}  Wayne Xin Zhao^{2}  Bryan Dai^{3} ^{1}Beihang University     ^{2}Renmin University     ^{3}IQuest Research cite52† GitHub†github.com cite53† Hugging Face†huggingface.co L69: ###### Abstract\nL70: Command-line interface (CLI) agents increasingly execute longer-horizon tasks, yet existing benchmarks either assume that user instructions remain fixed throughout execution or evaluate task outcomes without capturing runtime collaboration quality. In this paper, we present REALCLI, a paired benchmark that unifies the evaluation of task execution and human-agent collaboration quality on realistic CLI tasks.\nL71: To comprehensively measure collaboration, REALCLI builds a five-dimensional framework, operationalized into 22 sub-dimensions and 51 interaction scenario templates. REALCLI provides two modes for paired comparison: REALCLI-EXEC supplies complete specifications upfront, whereas REALCLI-COLLAB introduces a scripted user that delivers progressive information, corrections, constraint revisions, and authorization decisions.\nL88: To ground these dimensions in real-world CLI usage, we analyze recurring collaboration needs and failure patterns in anonymized interaction logs, distilling 22 sub-dimensions that are operationalized through 51 interaction scenario templates and six trajectory-level checks. Each scenario specifies the behavior to be evaluated and the evidence required, making collaboration quality observable and auditable.\nL89: We further introduce RealCLI, a paired benchmark that evaluates both task execution and collaboration quality on realistic CLI tasks. RealCLI contains 101 tasks spanning 10 CLI categories and 52 subdomains, from local operations to complex workflows coordinating multiple constraints and deliverables. Each task has two modes sharing the task goal, environment, and verifier: RealCLI-Exec gives the complete specification upfront, while RealCLI-Collab introduces a scripted user during execution.\nL90: In RealCLI-Collab, information slices release the specification progressively, and interaction probes trigger clarification, correction, constraint-revision, and authorization scenarios according to execution states. Both modes judge task outcomes from terminal environment states; RealCLI-Collab additionally scores five-dimensional collaboration behaviors from interaction traces and environment evidence.\nL91: This pairing enables direct comparison between autonomous and collaborative execution, revealing not only how user participation changes task outcomes, but also when task effectiveness and collaboration quality align or diverge.\nL92: We systematically evaluate 33 configurations composed of 11 frontier models and three mainstream agent harnesses. Our main findings are as follows. First, RealCLI poses a substantial challenge to frontier agents. The best configuration attains an average score of only 52.7%, and task effectiveness diverges sharply from collaboration quality, as high task scores do not imply high collaboration quality. Second, user interaction is not a reliable gain.\nL93: Overall pass rates remain essentially unchanged, benefits vary by task type and model, and the additional execution effort does not consistently convert into task progress. Third, collaboration ability is consistently imbalanced across the five dimensions.\nL94: Agents perform relatively well on understandability and on observation and recovery, but remain weak on steerability, trust calibration, and safety and risk awareness, and models with identical task success rates can exhibit sharply different collaboration profiles. These results suggest that the next advance of CLI agents depends not only on stronger autonomous execution but also on understandable, steerable, and trustworthy collaboration.\nL190: Interactive Instance Synthesis. We augment each validated base instance with an interaction layer supporting gradual information disclosure and scenario-driven probes across five stages.\nL191: \nL192: (1) Scenario Assignment. For each base task, we assign six distinct collaboration scenarios based on task semantics, execution characteristics, and predefined templates, while balancing coverage across task types and the five-dimensional collaboration framework.\nL193: (2) Interactive Script Authoring. This phase produces an interaction script comprising an opening message, information slices, scenario probes, and checklist items. The opening message provides partial information, while information slices progressively disclose requirements and context. Each probe specifies a target collaboration behavior, expected evidence, and linked checklist items.\nL194: We render each probe as a user message presented to an uninformed agent, revising any probes that prematurely leak their evaluation intent.\nL195: (3) Script Validation. Static Checks verify script structure, file references, and alignment among scenarios, probes, and checklist items. Semantic Review assesses task–scenario fit, dialogue naturalness, and evidence traceability. Failed scripts return to authoring for revision.\nL196: (4) End-to-End Pilot. A target agent completes the task alongside a user simulator. Judge Review examines the resulting trajectory scenario by scenario to confirm that each intended collaboration capability is successfully elicited and measurable.\nL197: (5) Final Curation. Candidates passing the pilot are submitted to human expert review and difficulty filtering. Instances that successfully pass final curation retain their base task artifacts, append the specific files required for user interaction, and enter the delivery pool.\nL198: \nL199: Figure cite77†3 summarizes RealCLI’s statistics across 101 tasks, spanning 10 top-level categories and 52 subdomains, 51 collaboration scenario templates, and 1,658 evaluation checklist items.\nL200: Figure 3: Statistics dashboard of RealCLI. Fig. (a) shows the task categories and their subdomains. Fig. (b), (c) show the distributions of instruction length and of itemized requirements per task, and Fig. (d) the number of distinct file paths cited per instruction. Fig. (e) shows how the task verifiers are realized. Fig. (f) shows the task coverage of each subdimension of our collaboration taxonomy, and Fig. (g) the distribution of tasks over the scenarios.\nL201: Fig. (h) shows the evidence source of the checklist items, and Fig. (i) the per-task spread of the four interaction item kinds.\nL202: ## 4 EXPERIMENTS\nL203: ### 4.1 experimental Setup\nL204: Table 3: RealCLI main results on the held-out split across three agent scaffolds. Subscripts on $R_{collab}$ and $J_{collab}$ indicate performance changes relative to the $R_{exec}$ baseline (green positive, red negative). Bold and underline denote the highest and second-highest scores, respectively.\nL205: Model  | Claude Code  | Terminus-2  | Codex  | Avg.\nL206:  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  |\nL207: Closed-source models\nL208: ---\nL209: Claude Opus 5  | 58.2  | 58.4_{+0.2}  | 27.4_{-30.8}  | 65.4  | 62.4_{-3.0}  | 37.6_{-27.9}  | 46.5  | 55.5_{+9.0}  | 31.5_{-15.0}  | 49.2\nL210: Claude Opus 4.8  | 54.5  | 53.5_{-1.0}  | 18.3_{-36.3}  | 52.5  | 53.5_{+1.0}  | 21.1_{-31.4}  | 40.6  | 50.0_{+9.4}  | 23.0_{-17.6}  | 40.8\nL211: GPT-5.6 Sol  | 44.6  | 45.5_{+0.9}  | 6.7_{-37.9}  | 51.5  | 47.5_{-4.0}  | 9.1_{-42.4}  | 58.5  | 49.5_{-9.0}  | 8.5_{-50.0}  | 35.7\nL212: