 # RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation of\nL67: Human–Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents\nL68: Yunqi Liu^{3*}  Peiyu Wang^{3*}  Bingli Wang^{3*}  Leo Luo^{3*}  Xuanwu Song^{3*} Xudong Ma^{3}  Hongxin Li^{3}  Yaxin Du^{3}  Weicheng Gu^{3}  Fanglin Xu^{3}  Yi Xing^{3} XiangLong Liu^{1}  Jujie He^{3}  Zhange Zhang^{3}  Huatong Song^{3}  Zhaoyi Li^{3} Deyang Kong^{3}  Chuan Hao^{3}  Peihao Wu^{3}  Mingjie Tang^{3}  Ran Tao^{3} Jian Yang^{1†}  Wayne Xin Zhao^{2}  Bryan Dai^{3} ^{1}Beihang University     ^{2}Renmin University     ^{3}IQuest Research cite52† GitHub†github.com cite53† Hugging Face†huggingface.co L129: For example, one scenario has the user revise an existing requirement mid-execution, testing whether the agent adjusts subsequent work accordingly; another asks the agent to bundle sensitive files under the pretext of daily work habits, testing whether it recognizes the potential leak. See Section cite12†3 for framework construction and Appendix cite24†B for all subdimensions and scenario templates.\nL130: Table 2: Overview of collaboration dimensions (Sub.: subdimensions, Tpl.: scenario templates).\nL131: Dimension  | Evaluation focus  | Sub. / Tpl.\nL132: --- | --- | ---\nL133: U nderstandability (U)  | Legibility of plans, execution state, and outcomes  | 4 / 12\nL134: S teerability (S)  | User intervention, redirection, and constraint persistence  | 5 / 11\nL135: O bservation & R ecovery (OR)  | State verification and recovery from failures  | 3 / 9\nL136: T rust C alibration (TC)  | Accurate disclosure of risks, limits, and task status  | 5 / 12\nL137: S afety & R isk Awareness (SR)  | User control over high-impact or unauthorized actions  | 5 / 13\nL138: ### 2.3 Script-Driven User Simulation\nL139: \nL140: RealCLI uses a task-level user script to control how task information enters the interaction and when the user participates. Its core consists of information slices and interaction probes: the former governs the progressive release of task information, while the latter introduces collaboration situations during execution. For task $x$, the simulator at the $k$-th user interaction is defined as\nL141:  | $$\\mathcal{U}_{x}^{k}=\\left(c_{x},\\mathcal{I}_{x},\\mathcal{P}_{x},z_{k}\\right).$$  |  | (3)\nL142: \nL143: where $c_{x}$ specifies the user role and the opening and closing of the interaction, $\\mathcal{I}_{x}$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{x}$ denote the information slices and interaction probes, and $z_{k}$ denotes the current runtime state.\nL144: Information Slices. $\\mathcal{I}_{x}$ decomposes the complete task specification $\\omega$ into slices released progressively during execution. Slices are either provided in response to agent inquiries or proactively supplied by the simulator.\nL145: Interaction Probes. $\\mathcal{P}_{x}$ instantiates the scenario templates of the five-dimensional collaboration framework into probes tied to the current task. Each probe contains a scripted user intervention with its trigger condition, expected behavior, and evidence requirements. The trigger condition determines when the intervention enters the execution trajectory, and the evidence requirements specify how the agent’s response is verified.\nL146: At the $k$-th interaction, the simulator selects the applicable information slices and probes based on the preceding execution trajectory and state $z_{k}$, generates the user message, and updates the state. In this way, the timing of user interventions varies with the execution trajectory, while task semantics, key messages, and evaluation criteria remain fixed.\nL147: ### 2.4 Evaluation Metrics\nL148: \nL149: The evaluation framework of REALCLI consists of two independent axes: Task Success and Collaboration Quality. To measure the agent’s ability to balance both, we further introduce the Joint Collaboration Score.\nL150: Task Success. Task success measures whether the agent’s execution result satisfies the task goal and acceptance criteria. Let $\\mathcal{X}$ denote the evaluation task set. For task $x$, the task verifier $V_{x}$ produces a binary outcome from the terminal environment state $s_{T}^{m}$ under mode $m$. The average task reward under mode $m$ is defined as\nL151: \nL152:  | $$R_{m}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{X}|}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{X}}V_{x}(s_{T}^{m}),\\qquad V_{x}(s_{T}^{m})\\in\\{0,1\\}.$$  |  | (4)\nL153: $R_{\\mathrm{EXEC}}$ and $R_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$ denote the average task scores under EXEC and COLLAB, respectively.\nL154: \nL155: Collaboration Quality. To accurately measure the agent’s collaboration proficiency during dynamic interactions, we construct a four-level bottom-up aggregation logic. This logic aggregates micro-behavioral checks into a macro-level composite capability score ($S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$):\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: Level 1: Task-Level Scenario Perfection. For a specific collaboration scenario $q$ triggered in task $t$, the agent must satisfy all applicable checklist items to receive a score of 1 (complete pass). Any omission or violation results in a score of 0. This strict all-or-nothing criterion ensures the flawless execution of individual collaborative behaviors.\nL159: \nL160:   * •\nL161: Level 2: Cross-Task Scenario Consistency. We evaluate the average pass rate of a scenario $q$ across the entire test set (i.e., all tasks where $q$ is triggered). To ensure the agent exhibits stable and reliable collaboration, we enforce a strict 75% threshold: the final score $\\hat{r}_{q}$ for the scenario is 1 (pass) if and only if its overall pass rate is $\\geq 0.75$; otherwise, it is 0.\nL162: \nL163:  | $$\\hat{r}_{q}=\\mathbb{I}\\left(\\text{pass rate of }q\\geq 0.75\\right)$$  |  | (5)\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: Level 3: Subdimension & Dimension Score. After determining the binary pass status of each scenario, we aggregate the scores upwards using equal-weight averaging:\nL167: \nL168:     1. 1.\nL169: \nL170: Subdimension Score ($s_{u}$): The arithmetic mean of the scenario scores ($\\hat{r}_{q}$) within the subdimension $u$.\nL171: \nL172:     2. 2.\nL173: Dimension Score ($s_{d}$): The score for each of the five core dimensions (Understandability $U$, Steerability $S$, Observation and Recovery $OR$, Trust Calibration $TC$, and Safety and Risk Awareness $SR$) is the arithmetic mean of its constituent subdimension scores ($s_{u}$).\nL174: \nL175:   * •\nL176: \nL177: Level 4: Composite Collaboration Score ($S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$). Finally, the composite collaboration score, which represents the global interaction quality, is the equal-weight average of the five core dimension scores:\nL193: (2) Interactive Script Authoring. This phase produces an interaction script comprising an opening message, information slices, scenario probes, and checklist items. The opening message provides partial information, while information slices progressively disclose requirements and context. Each probe specifies a target collaboration behavior, expected evidence, and linked checklist items.\nL194: We render each probe as a user message presented to an uninformed agent, revising any probes that prematurely leak their evaluation intent.\nL195: (3) Script Validation. Static Checks verify script structure, file references, and alignment among scenarios, probes, and checklist items. Semantic Review assesses task–scenario fit, dialogue naturalness, and evidence traceability. Failed scripts return to authoring for revision.\nL196: (4) End-to-End Pilot. A target agent completes the task alongside a user simulator. Judge Review examines the resulting trajectory scenario by scenario to confirm that each intended collaboration capability is successfully elicited and measurable.\nL197: (5) Final Curation. Candidates passing the pilot are submitted to human expert review and difficulty filtering. Instances that successfully pass final curation retain their base task artifacts, append the specific files required for user interaction, and enter the delivery pool.\nL198: \nL199: Figure cite77†3 summarizes RealCLI’s statistics across 101 tasks, spanning 10 top-level categories and 52 subdomains, 51 collaboration scenario templates, and 1,658 evaluation checklist items.\nL200: Figure 3: Statistics dashboard of RealCLI. Fig. (a) shows the task categories and their subdomains. Fig. (b), (c) show the distributions of instruction length and of itemized requirements per task, and Fig. (d) the number of distinct file paths cited per instruction. Fig. (e) shows how the task verifiers are realized. Fig. (f) shows the task coverage of each subdimension of our collaboration taxonomy, and Fig. (g) the distribution of tasks over the scenarios.\nL201: Fig. (h) shows the evidence source of the checklist items, and Fig. (i) the per-task spread of the four interaction item kinds.\nL202: ## 4 EXPERIMENTS\nL203: ### 4.1 experimental Setup\nL204: Table 3: RealCLI main results on the held-out split across three agent scaffolds. Subscripts on $R_{collab}$ and $J_{collab}$ indicate performance changes relative to the $R_{exec}$ baseline (green positive, red negative). Bold and underline denote the highest and second-highest scores, respectively.\nL205: Model  | Claude Code  | Terminus-2  | Codex  | Avg.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNot All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41244view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"pattern\":\"Appendix A\"}); Total lines: 472\nL342:   * Zheng et al. (2023) L. Zheng, W. Chiang, Y. Sheng, S. Zhuang, Z. Wu, Y. Zhuang, Z. Lin, Z. Li, D. Li, E. Xing, H. Zhang, J. Gonzalez, and I. Stoica Judging llm-as-a-judge with mt-bench and chatbot arena. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, A. Oh, T. Naumann, A. Globerson, K. Saenko, M. Hardt, and S. Levine (Eds.), Vol. 36, pp. 46595–46623. External Links: cite172†Document†dx.doi.org , cite173†Link†proceedings.neurips.cc Cited by: cite141†§1 , cite116†§5 .\nL343:   * Zhou et al. (2026) S. Zhou, R. Ling, J. Chen, X. Wang, T. Fan, and H. Wang When more thinking hurts: overthinking in LLM test-time compute scaling. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026, M. Liakata, V. P. Moreira, J. Zhang, and D. Jurgens (Eds.), San Diego, California, United States, pp. 23967–23977. External Links: cite174†Link†aclanthology.org , cite175†Document†dx.doi.org , ISBN 979-8-89176-395-1 Cited by: cite124†§1 , cite122†§1 , cite116†§5 .\nL344:   * Zhu et al. (2025) X. Zhu, C. Zhang, T. Stafford, N. Collier, and A. Vlachos Conformity in large language models. In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pp. 3854–3872. Cited by: cite115†§5 .\nL345: ## Appendix A Appendix\nL346: \nL347: ### A.1 Dataset Statistics and Sampling\nL348: \nL349: We evaluate seven benchmark families spanning the four reasoning domains introduced in the main text. All evaluation subsets are fixed before inference, and the same canonical inputs are shared across methods and base models.\nL350: ##### Knowledge-intensive reasoning.\nL351: \nL352: MMLU-Pro (cite83†Wang et al., 2024 ) contains 12,032 test questions from 14 broad knowledge domains. We sample 25 questions from each domain, yielding a balanced 350-question subset. For GPQA (cite84†Rein et al., 2024 ), we use the full Diamond split of 198 questions. The split contains 19 Biology, 93 Chemistry, and 86 Physics questions.\nL353: ##### Contextual and multi-hop reasoning.\nL354: For DetectBench (cite85†Gu et al., 2024 ), we use 279 questions sampled without replacement from the 3,928 questions across the official train, dev, test, test-hard, and test-distract files. For MuSiQue (cite75†Trivedi et al., 2022 ), we sample 300 answerable questions from the 2,417-question MuSiQue-Ans development pool; the resulting subset contains 163 two-hop, 84 three-hop, and 53 four-hop questions.\nL355: For 2WikiMultiHopQA (cite86†Ho et al., 2020 ), we sample 200 questions from the 12,576-example development split. This subset contains 44 bridge-comparison, 48 comparison, 83 compositional, and 25 inference questions.\nL356: ##### Mathematical reasoning.\nL357: \nL358: MATH (cite87†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ) covers seven subjects: Algebra, Counting and Probability, Geometry, Intermediate Algebra, Number Theory, Prealgebra, and Precalculus. We independently sample 100 questions from each of difficulty Levels 3, 4, and 5 from the official test split. This produces three equally sized subsets, or 300 questions in total, and balances the evaluation across difficulty levels.\nL359: ##### Narrative and soft reasoning.\nL360: \nL361: MuSR (cite88†Sprague et al., 2024 ) comprises murder mysteries, object placements, and team-allocation tasks. We sample 100 questions from each of the object-placement and team-allocation task families, yielding 200 evaluation questions in total.\nL362: ### A.2 SFT and DPO Baselines\nL363: For each dataset we construct a separate training set, sample the base model eight times per question, and label each sample as correct or incorrect. SFT trains the base model for two epochs on the correct samples; DPO then trains for one epoch on preference pairs in which a correct sample is preferred over an incorrect sample of the same question.\n"
